course: dictionary
lesson: 12909
title: When things escalate
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Escalate is a verb. It means to grow bigger, worse or more serious. «The argument escalated into a fight.» A small disagreement grew until it became a fight. The noun is escalation, which means the growing itself.
explain: Look for the clue word «into». When something escalates, it often turns into something bigger. «The quarrel escalated into shouting.» Ask yourself: did the problem get bigger or worse? If yes, escalate is probably the word.
explain: Good friends and wise grown-ups can stop things escalating. To de-escalate is to calm things down, listen and walk away. In everyday life, prices can escalate too, rising fast. If a problem at school or online escalates, tell a trusted adult straight away.
fact: Escalate means to become bigger, worse or more serious.
fact: Escalate is a verb: escalates, escalated, escalating. The noun is escalation.
fact: Escalate is often followed by «into», as in «escalate into a fight».
fact: To de-escalate means to calm a problem down.
fact: Escalate can also mean to pass a problem to a more senior person, as in «escalate a complaint».
exercise: «The argument escalated into a fight.» What does escalated mean? | *Became bigger and more serious | Ended with a hug | Was forgotten | Became calm and quiet
exercise: Mia says her teacher should escalate her complaint to the head teacher. What does escalate mean here? | Say it very quietly | Forget about it | *Pass it to a more senior person | Write it on a card
exercise: Which word means to calm a quarrel down? | Escalate | *De-escalate | Scale | Escalation
test: «Prices escalated fast last winter.» What does escalated mean here? | Were given away free | Stayed the same | *Rose quickly | Went down
test: Your friend says a problem at school is escalating. Who should you tell? | Nobody, it will fix itself | *A trusted adult | The dog next door | The ice cream van
exam: Which word best completes this sentence? The small disagreement began to ___ into a big fight. | de-escalate | *escalate | escalator | apologise
